  "Question": "If the reciprocal of \\(x+1\\) is \\(x-1,\\) then \\(x\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(-1\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\pm1\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The reciprocal of \\(x+1\\) is \\(1/(x+1)\\), so \\(x\\ne-1\\) and\n\\[\n\\frac1{x+1}=x-1.\n\\]\nMultiplying by the nonzero denominator \\(x+1\\) gives\n\\[\n1=(x+1)(x-1)=x^2-1.\n\\]\nThus \\(x^2=2\\), whose two real solutions are \\(x=\\pm\\sqrt2\\). Neither root is \\(-1\\), and each satisfies \\((x+1)(x-1)=1\\), so dividing by \\(x+1\\) verifies the original reciprocal condition.\n\nNeither root is \\(0\\), \\(1\\), or \\(-1\\). Therefore \\(x=\\pm\\sqrt2\\), and the answer is choice E, none of these.",
